  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Examining Feature Placement**\n\nOkay, I\'ve confirmed the plan: the random shuffle icon \360\237\224\200 goes directly into `PlaylistDetailView.tsx`. Now, I\'m diving deeper into coding the \"play randomly\" feature within that same component. I need to be sure the functionality is implemented correctly.\n\n\n**Adding Random Play Button**\n\nI\'ve decided to integrate a \"Play Randomly\" button alongside the existing play/pause button. This new button will use `onPlayRandomly` as its action, and will live as a flex item within the same UI container. I\'m focusing now on the visual styling to make it seamless. The objective is to keep it visually consistent.\n\n\n**Implementing Random Play Logic**\n\nI\'ve got the button\'s appearance finalized, and now I\'m focused on the button\'s action. I\'ve decided the best approach is to define `onPlayPlaylistRandomly(playlist: Playlist)` in `App.tsx` and pass it down. This function will elegantly shuffle the playlist\'s songs, update the `playQueue`, and set the first song as the `currentSong` and set the `isShuffle` flag to true. This way we ensure the \"Play Randomly\" option works as intended. This is so elegant!\n\n\n**Planning Database Persistence**\n\nI\'m now outlining the steps to make our music app state persistent. First, I\'ll initialize the `songs` and `playlists` states in `App.tsx` from `localStorage`, ensuring data survives page reloads. This will require some careful error handling, but the benefit is obvious. The goal is a seamless user experience!\n\n\n**Defining Initial State**\n\nOkay, I\'ve decided the initial state for playlists will be an empty array (`[]`) to start. This way, the user sees a clean slate, encouraging them to create their own playlists easily. I\'m now transitioning to the \"Add Song\" creation modal feature. The goal is a clean, minimal, initial state.\n\n\\n\\n\n\nMy next step is creating an \"Add Song\" Form/Modal component. I\'m thinking of adding a modal trigger in the Header/Library view so users can add tracks through selection or drag-and-drop. I\'m very excited about making sure users can upload and listen to their *own* audio files, not just demos. I\'m also now planning to use `URL.createObjectURL(file)` to get playable Blob URLs for the uploaded tracks, which will allow for real audio playback in the browser. I am thinking of using local storage for storing audio metadata, but I also plan to describe local storage limits for audio files to manage user expectations.\n\n\n**Planning Cloud Backup**\n\nI\'m now thinking about cloud saving. I am planning to integrate Firebase Firestore and Firebase Storage for cloud backups to save user playlists. I intend to ask the user\'s permission first, in the final message.\n\n\\n\\n\nI\'ve already decided how to add a beautifully themed \"Play Randomly\" button with a shuffle symbol \360\237\224\200 in `PlaylistDetailView` next to the central Play button. It will shuffle the playlist\'s tracks and start playing. In the same view, I will add a \"Manage Library Tracks\" or \"Available Songs to Add\" section at the bottom, matching the theme. Finally, I\'ve got to fix the light mode text colors by replacing hardcoded white text with theme-aware classes to ensure readability in all modes.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will write the `/src/components/AddSongModal.tsx` component. It will allow users to upload real audio files (.mp3, .wav, .m4a, etc.) and cover images (.jpg, .png) directly from their PC or mobile. This modal uses standard React HTML5 file-reader APIs and creates dynamic playable Blob URLs using `URL.createObjectURL(file)` so that uploaded songs actually play sound in the browser player."
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 8
      nanos: 285100694
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
