  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Examining AnalyticsView.tsx**\n\nI\'m making good progress! I\'ve started examining `AnalyticsView.tsx`, as requested. I\'m focusing on ensuring it receives the `playlists` array, as that\'s crucial for the requested analysis of the selected playlist in H-FI Analytics. The user\'s directive is front-of-mind.\n\n\n**Implementing Playlist Selection**\n\nI\'m now focused on implementing a user-friendly playlist selection in `AnalyticsView.tsx`. The goal is a clean dropdown allowing the user to choose which playlist to analyze, with the component receiving the playlists array. I\'m aiming for resilience: if the `playlists` array is empty, I\'ll calculate analytics based on the entire `songs` library instead. This guarantees functionality regardless of the user\'s playlist setup.\n\n\n**Calculating Genre Percentages**\n\nI\'ve moved on to calculating actual genre percentages! Now that the playlist selection is working, I\'m focusing on dynamic calculations. Specifically, I\'m implementing the genre breakdown for the *selected* playlist. I\'ve written some TypeScript to filter songs for selected playlists, count genres, and compute percentages. This dynamic approach guarantees I\'m adhering to the user\'s focus on playlist-specific analytics.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will rewrite `src/components/AnalyticsView.tsx` to:\n1. Accept the available `songs` list and public `playlists` array dynamically as props.\n2. Render a themed dropdown search selectors for choosing which playlist to analyze (falling back to analyzing the entire website library if there are no playlists currently created).\n3. Compute the active **genre breakdown percentages** completely dynamically based on the actual tracks loaded in that selected playlist instead of showing hardcoded mock entries.\n4. Update the center gauge metric of the Pie Chart to show the actual calculated **top genre** for the analysed playlist.\n5. Fix light mode readability by changing all fixed `text-white` classes in title tags and grids to use theme-adaptive elements."
